  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"117\" data-end=\"358\"><strong data-start=\"117\" data-end=\"148\">Factor XII (Hageman factor)</strong> is a clotting factor involved in the <strong data-start=\"186\" data-end=\"207\">intrinsic pathway</strong> of the coagulation cascade. It plays a role in initiating clot formation <strong data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"293\">in vitro</strong>, although it is <strong data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"357\">not essential for normal hemostasis in vivo</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"360\" data-end=\"409\">The <strong data-start=\"364\" data-end=\"392\">Factor XII Activity test</strong> is performed to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"411\" data-end=\"615\">\n<li data-start=\"411\" data-end=\"483\">\n<p data-start=\"413\" data-end=\"483\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"483\">prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"484\" data-end=\"520\">\n<p data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"520\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"520\">Factor XII deficiency</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"521\" data-end=\"615\">\n<p data-start=\"523\" data-end=\"615\">Distinguish between true coagulation disorders and <strong data-start=\"574\" data-end=\"615\">asymptomatic laboratory abnormalities</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"617\" data-end=\"785\">Despite its role in coagulation tests, <strong data-start=\"656\" data-end=\"705\">Factor XII deficiency does not cause bleeding</strong>, but has been associated with <strong data-start=\"736\" data-end=\"768\">increased risk of thrombosis</strong> in some studies.</p>\n<p data-start=\"617\" data-end=\"785\">Elevated Factor XII levels are <strong data-start=\"875\" data-end=\"915\">not commonly associated with disease</strong>, but may be seen in:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute phase response</strong></td>\n<td>As an acute phase reactant, Factor XII may rise during inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hypercoagulable states (rare)</strong></td>\n<td>Some studies suggest elevated Factor XII may correlate with thrombosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Estrogen therapy / Pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td>Hormonal influence may raise Factor XII levels transiently</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"617\" data-end=\"785\">&nbsp;</p>",
